  "strategy": "### Approach and Rationale\n**Central Idea:**  \n Represent step functions as finite blends of scaling functions on a dyadic grid. Then impose a structured sparsity requirement to control degrees of freedom and divorce the optimization landscape from high-dimensional plateaus.\n\n**Bottleneck Addressed:**  \nExisting gradient-based methods are grid-impoverished away from optimal shifts and overly smooth high-dimensional manifolds. We address this by:  \n- Indexing overlapping tri-directional patches (time-shift, level, scale, direction) independently during multi-level constrained optimization  \n- Using lifting schemes to enforce structure preservation through interval constraints  \n- Deploying differentiable projection to clip outputs without perturbing losses\n\n**Outcome Hype:**  \nThis should identify boundary-crossing solutions where sufficiently light sparsity and dyadic structure cuts through the common thicket of annoying local minima.\n\n### Mathematical Specification\n**Representation:**  \nDefine  adaptive dyadic scaling functions $\\phi_j$ (where j indexes both spatial level and temporal patch) and projection  \n$h(x) = \\sum_{j=1}^N c_j \\phi_j(x)$, requiring $\\int_0^2 h dx = 0.5$\n\n**Objective:**  \n$$\\max_{k\\in\\mathbb{Z}} \\int_0^2 h(x) \\cdot (1-h(x+k)) dx \\quad \\text{over fixed support } [\\min(x,x+k),\\max(x,x+k)]$$\n\n**Constraints:**  \n1. $0 \\leq \\phi_j(x) \\leq 1$  \n2. $\\sum_{j=1}^N c_j \\phi_j(x) \\in [0,1]$  \n3. $\\int_0^2 h(x) dx = 0.5$\n\n**Optimization Variables:**  \nParameters $(c_j)_{j=1}^N$ where \n- N is chosen as $O(\\log(\\max_k |\\int h(1-h(k))dx|)$\n- $\\phi_j(x)$ are defined on dyadic intervals of total length 2/3 to fit shift window \u223c \u00b10.60 of domain\n\n**Differentiability:**  \nObjective is twice-differentiable with respect to c_j due to multiple layers of projections for constraint satisfaction. The gradient is sparse as it involves only adjacent $\\phi_j$.\n\n### Implementation Procedure\n**Phase 0 (Validation):**  \n1. Confirm `initial_h_values` is NumPy array with length N_init (if not, use fallback interpolation)  \n2. Scale resolution by factor $r \\in [1,50]$ with dyadic adaptivity\n\n**Core Optimization:**  \nUse projected ADAM for constraint satisfaction with custom loss:  \n$$\\mathcal{L} = \\max_k \\left( \\int h(x) [1-h(x+k)] dx \\right) + \\lambda ||c||_1$$\n\n**Constraint Projection:**  \nParametrize conserved momentum by $\\mu = \\frac{\\int_0^2 h dx}{0.5}$ (shifted integral). Projection operation:  \n$$\\text{project}(h) = \\min_{g \\in L^2} \\{g: |g|_2^2 \\text{dist}(h,\\{g: [\\cdot]\\}) \\}$$\n (For dyadic representation, this involves clipping coefficients until satisfied)\n\n**Projected Gradient Step:**  \nUpdate c_new = c_old + $\\alpha \\overrightarrow{\\nabla h}$  \nthen project h into [0,1] by fixing g = $\\sum_{j=1}^N c_j \\phi_j$ to [0,1] using:  \n$$c_j^{new} = \\max(0,\\min(1, c_j^{old}))$$\n\n**Objective Evaluation:**  \nNeed to approximate  \n$\\max_k \\int h(x) [1-h(x+k)] dx$  \nby sparse grid correlation through temporal lifting but only at candidate time shifts derived from peak signal.\n\n### Search Choices and Variations\n**Dimensionality Control:**  \nChoose N as closest power of 2 to $10 \\cdot 2^{d/2}$ where d is decades of improvement needed (initial is 0.38096 target 0.38085 \u21d2 log2(1.15/1000)\u2248-12, thus use N=4096).\n\n**Parameter Tuning:**  \nUse Bayesian optimization for $\\lambda \\in (10^{-4}, 0.1)$  \n\n**Representation Strategy:**  \nUse $ \\phi_j^b(x) = \\mathcal{Q}_{\\text{work}} \\cdot \\text{DyadicWindow}(b)$ where b=0..N-1 indexes positions\n\n**Constraint Handling:**  \nImplement soft constraints with KL divergence from ideal. Gradually tighten during optimization.\n\n### Budget and Resource Plan\n**Process Timeline:**  \n1. Start with N_min = 128, maximize via data-driven residual scaling  \n2. Total iteration count is 3*N_per_scale * (factor) where factor is selected based on wall-clock performance\n\n**Estimate:**  \nOn a 2.0 GHz machine, optimizing with 512 bases (N) takes \u223c6s/10k steps. Best-case, we can do 207k steps in 5 minutes.\n\n**Failure Grace:**  \nIf loss increases during plateau phase, switch to convex surrogate. If solver stuck, restart from median values.\n\n### Validation and Return Contract\n**Validation Checks:**  \n1. $ \\text{True} \\iff 0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1 \\forall x $  \n2. $ \\int_0^2 h dx \\approx 0.5 $  \n3. Compute C_5 using direct correlation (not approximation)  \n4. Compare against all recorded previous runs, take best if value is worse (in this case, lower is better)\n\n**Implementation Note:**  \nAll base function definitions must be adjusted to ensure periodicity by consistency with dyadic grid. The projection must strictly enforce $[0,1]$ by clipping coefficients but also preserving $||\\phi||_2$.\n\nOptimization depends on avoiding pseudo-convergence. Monitor loss and gradient sparsity. When gradient becomes clean and loss plateaus, declare success.\n\nReturn the Pareto-optimal solution (lowest C_5 value across all runs) with associated h_values and N_points used.",
